(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-cms-exam-6-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 6 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 60-year-old man with type 2 diabetes treated with metformin received several liters of normal saline during abdominal surgery. He has had no diarrhea. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.33 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 33 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 17 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 141 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 114 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which explains this blood gas?","choices":["His metformin","Postoperative hypoventilation","The large saline infusion","Diabetic ketoacidosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Low pH with a low bicarbonate is metabolic acidosis; the gap is 141 - (114 + 17) = 10, within 9 to 15, with a high chloride: a normal-gap, hyperchloremic acidosis. Saline infusion is a listed normal-gap cause (chloride retention). — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with severe obesity and a high fever from pneumonia has a carbon dioxide of 62 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). What usually causes a raised carbon dioxide like his?","choices":["Hyperventilation","Excess bicarbonate retained by the kidneys","Under-excretion from hypoventilation","Excess production from the fever"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypercapnia is usually the result of under-excretion (hypoventilation), not excess production. At steady state carbon dioxide production matches excretion, so a high level means ventilation is not keeping up. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which presentation separates pneumonia from acute bronchitis?","choices":["Low-grade fever with body aches","Central substernal wheeze","Higher fever with tachypnea","Cough with colored sputum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Apart from walking pneumonia, pneumonia presents more severely than bronchitis: higher fevers and chills, pleuritic or atypical chest pain, a productive cough and shortness of breath with tachypnea. Bronchitis tends to bring a low-grade fever. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 27, 16"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman on a nasal cannula at 6 L/min without humidification develops nosebleeds and a sore, crusted nose. Which hazard of oxygen therapy is this?","choices":["Vasoconstriction","Hypercapnia","Mucosal irritation","Oxygen toxicity of the lungs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. She has been on 6 L/min through a nasal cannula without humidification, and now has nosebleeds and a sore, crusted nose. Dry oxygen irritating the nasal lining is mucosal irritation, a listed hazard. Humidity is used when the flow is greater than 4 L/min. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 9"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with a long-term tracheostomy after head and neck cancer needs humidified supplemental oxygen at home. Which interface delivers air-entrainment nebulizer oxygen to his airway?","choices":["Trach collar","Simple mask","Nasal cannula","Open face mask"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. He has a long-term tracheostomy and needs humidified oxygen delivered by an air-entrainment nebulizer. The trach collar is the air-entrainment nebulizer interface that fits over a tracheostomy, so it delivers to his airway directly. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 37-year-old man has acute bronchitis and is not given an antibiotic. He asks what to expect from his illness. Which counseling statement is accurate?","choices":["The cough often lasts 2 to 3 weeks","The cough should clear in 3 days","An antibiotic would halve the cough","Green sputum means it is bacterial"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute bronchitis is usually a self-limited viral infection, and the cough commonly lasts 2 to 3 weeks and may persist longer. Setting that expectation, and explaining that antibiotics do not significantly shorten the illness, is how unneeded antibiotic requests are reduced. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 16, 17, 18, 21"},{"q":"Which lung exam finding reflects a flattened diaphragm in this disease?","choices":["Loud heart sounds at the apex","Neck veins that distend on inspiration","Lower intercostal retraction on inspiration","Hyperresonance confined to one apex"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Paradoxical retraction of the lower intercostal spaces during inspiration reflects a flat diaphragm from hyperinflation. Other lung findings include decreased breath sounds in some areas with wheezing or crackles. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has spirometry for suspected asthma. Her forced expiratory volume in 1 second is reduced with a reduced ratio, and it improves 14% after albuterol. How should this response be read?","choices":["As too small to count","As a positive bronchodilator response","As proof of fixed obstruction","As a reason to skip spirometry next time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Improvement of 12% or more in the forced expiratory volume in 1 second after a bronchodilator is a positive response, and her 14% rise meets it. A positive response on top of reduced baseline values strongly supports the diagnosis of asthma. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 52, 53, 54"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman with a normal-gap metabolic acidosis has a bicarbonate of 18 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). By the Winter formula, what carbon dioxide range is expected with appropriate compensation?","choices":["24 to 28 mm Hg","25 to 29 mm Hg","33 to 37 mm Hg","36 to 44 mm Hg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x 18 + 8 = 27 + 8 = 35, plus or minus 2, so 33 to 37 mm Hg. A value above 37 would add a respiratory acidosis; below 33, a respiratory alkalosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 57-year-old smoker has chronic cough and mild breathlessness. Post-bronchodilator spirometry shows a ratio of forced expiratory volume in 1 second to forced vital capacity of 0.76. How should this result be read?","choices":["It proves asthma","It does not confirm the disease","It confirms the disease","It shows grade 2 disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis requires a post-bronchodilator ratio below 0.70, and his is 0.76. Chronic bronchitis without airflow obstruction is not chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, though smoking cessation remains essential for him. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 6, 26"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease says she walks slower than friends her age on level ground because of breathlessness, but she does not need to stop. Which modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade fits?","choices":["Grade 0","Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 3"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Grade 2 is walking slower than people of the same age on the level because of breathlessness, or having to stop for breath at one's own pace on the level. A grade of 2 or more counts as more symptoms. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man has latent tuberculosis infection after household exposure to his brother, who has pulmonary tuberculosis; disease has been excluded in him. Besides possible drug interactions, what must be checked before a latent-infection regimen is chosen?","choices":["A repeat skin test in 8 weeks","A blood assay to confirm the skin test","Culture conversion on sputum","The source case's drug resistance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Before a latent-infection regimen is chosen, interactions, pregnancy considerations and source-case resistance are checked, after disease has been excluded. His brother is the source case, so the brother's resistance pattern shapes which of the short regimens fits. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 37, 23"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with asthma has had fever, myalgias, cough and a sore throat for 30 hours, and influenza is confirmed by molecular testing. She is well enough for outpatient care. Which antiviral should be avoided?","choices":["Intravenous peramivir","Oseltamivir","Baloxavir","Inhaled zanamivir"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled zanamivir is avoided in asthma and chronic obstructive lung disease because of the risk of bronchospasm. Her asthma makes it the antiviral to avoid; oseltamivir, single-dose baloxavir within 48 hours, or intravenous peramivir remain options for uncomplicated outpatient influenza. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"What is the preferred treatment for psittacosis in adults?","choices":["Doxycycline","Amoxicillin","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","A macrolide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Doxycycline is preferred in adults, started promptly when psittacosis is strongly suspected; improvement often begins within 48 to 72 hours. If tetracyclines are unsuitable, a macrolide is discussed with infectious disease, though macrolide failures have occurred. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has a post-bronchodilator ratio of 0.48 and a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 26% predicted. Which airflow grade fits?","choices":["Grade 1 (mild)","Grade 2 (moderate)","Grade 3 (severe)","Grade 4 (very severe)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With the ratio below 0.70, a post-bronchodilator 1-second volume of 26% predicted is below 30%, grade 4, very severe. Grade 3 covers 30% to 49%. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which term names a pathologic condition with excessively alkaline body fluids or tissues?","choices":["Hypocapnia","Alkalemia","Acidosis","Alkalosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Alkalosis is the pathologic condition of excessively alkaline body fluids or tissues. The resulting blood finding, a pH above 7.45, is called alkalemia, so the condition and the finding have different names. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man who drank methanol is obtunded. Arterial blood gas: pH 6.65 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 18 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 2 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Besides treating the poisoning, what does this pH warrant?","choices":["Paper bag rebreathing","No pH-directed therapy","Therapy to raise the serum pH","Waiting for respiratory compensation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A pH below 6.7 is unsustainable: it may cause cardiac hypocontractility and irritability, seizures and cerebral edema, and may warrant therapy to normalize the serum pH along with treatment of the underlying cause. His pH is 6.65. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 2-year-old boy lives with an uncle who was diagnosed with infectious pulmonary tuberculosis 6 weeks ago. The boy now has fever and cough, and his chest radiograph shows hilar adenopathy. Which stage of tuberculosis best fits?","choices":["Progressive primary tuberculosis","Contained primary infection","Latent tuberculosis infection","Reactivation (secondary) tuberculosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Progressive primary tuberculosis is disease that develops soon after initial infection when immune containment fails. Infancy and young childhood raise progression risk, his exposure is recent, and primary disease may show hilar or mediastinal adenopathy, infiltrates or effusion. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 9, 15, 28"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and few symptoms asks to be started on an inhaled corticosteroid by itself because a friend with asthma uses one. Which regimen should be avoided in him?","choices":["A single short-acting bronchodilator","An inhaled corticosteroid alone","A yearly influenza vaccine","Smoking cessation with counseling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In this disease, inhaled corticosteroids must be combined with a long-acting beta agonist, and then with a long-acting antimuscarinic as triple therapy, and cannot be used on their own. They also raise pneumonia risk. Alone, they are an asthma treatment. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man with asthma took aspirin at noon for a headache and developed facial flushing, a runny nose and wheezing at about 10 that night. He asks whether the aspirin could be responsible so many hours later. Which reply is accurate?","choices":["No, aspirin sensitivity causes rash alone","Yes, but just selective agents cause it","No, reactions occur within minutes","Yes, reactions can be delayed up to 12 hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In aspirin-sensitive asthma, symptoms may occur within minutes of aspirin ingestion or may be delayed up to 12 hours, and they include rhinorrhea, conjunctival irritation, facial flushing and wheezing. His reaction 10 hours after the dose fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which three conditions form the atopic triad?","choices":["Allergic rhinitis, sinusitis, and conjunctivitis","Asthma, eczema, and food poisoning","Asthma, nasal polyps, and aspirin sensitivity","Allergic rhinitis, asthma, and atopic dermatitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The atopic triad is allergic rhinitis, asthma and atopic dermatitis (eczema). Finding one prompts questions about the others, and in extrinsic asthma the exam may show swollen nasal mucosa, increased secretions or eczema even when the chest is clear. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which drugs make up the last 9 weeks of the 17-week rifapentine-moxifloxacin regimen?","choices":["Isoniazid, rifapentine, moxifloxacin","Isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide","Isoniazid, rifampin, ethambutol","Rifapentine, pyrazinamide, moxifloxacin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The 17-week regimen gives isoniazid, rifapentine, pyrazinamide and moxifloxacin for the first 8 weeks, then isoniazid, rifapentine and moxifloxacin for the next 9 weeks. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is reviewed for comorbidities that become more important with age. Which condition should be added to his screening at this age?","choices":["Osteoporosis and falls","Exercise-induced bronchospasm","Allergic rhinitis","Nasal polyps"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Comorbidities in older adults add osteoporosis, frailty, falls and cognitive decline to cardiovascular disease, metabolic disease and depression. Inhaled corticosteroid-related fracture risk adds to the concern about bone health. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 18, 53"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man who ingested ethylene glycol has an arterial pH of 6.62 (normal 7.35 to 7.45). Which complications does this pH threaten?","choices":["Hypokalemia from potassium entering cells","Reduced oxygen release from hemoglobin","Seizures, cerebral edema, cardiac irritability","Tetany from low ionized calcium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A pH below 6.7 is unsustainable and may cause cardiac hypocontractility and irritability, seizures, cerebral edema and metabolic disturbances; it may warrant therapy to normalize the pH along with treating the cause. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman with allergic asthma triggered by cat dander is considering allergen immunotherapy against that single allergen. Which statement about this therapy applies to her?","choices":["It works poorly against a single allergen","It can be self-given safely at home","It must be given in a controlled setting","It is reserved for non-allergic asthma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Immunotherapy may be considered when there are allergic triggers and is most useful against a single allergen, as for her cat dander. It must be done in a controlled environment because it can induce bronchoconstriction and may require urgent treatment of complications. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with community-acquired pneumonia is alert and oriented. Her blood urea nitrogen is 26 mg/dL, respiratory rate 24/min and blood pressure 128/76 mm Hg. What is her CURB-65 (confusion, urea, respiratory rate, blood pressure, age 65 or older) score?","choices":["1","2","3","4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Confusion 0 (alert and oriented) + urea 1 (26 mg/dL is above 20) + respiratory rate 0 (24 is below 30) + blood pressure 0 (systolic not below 90, diastolic not 60 or lower) + age 1 (72) = 2. A total of 2 is moderate severity, with a risk of death of 9%, and the pathway sends her to the hospital. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 29, 31"},{"q":"Which delivery device is classed as a high flow device?","choices":["Simple mask","Venturi mask","Non-rebreather mask","Nasal cannula"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The Venturi mask is classed as a high flow device, and it allows precise delivery of oxygen, with different ports changing the fraction delivered. The flow is adjusted from 4 to 10 L/min to give 24% to 50%. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man is confused after drinking antifreeze (ethylene glycol). Sodium 140 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), potassium 4.0 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L), chloride 102 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 8 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What is his anion gap?","choices":["30","34","38","46"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anion gap = sodium - (chloride + bicarbonate) = 140 - (102 + 8) = 140 - 110 = 30, above the normal 9 to 15: an elevated-gap metabolic acidosis, fitting ethylene glycol, the E of the mnemonic. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Recent hotel or cruise ship travel raises concern for which pneumonia pathogen?","choices":["Blastomyces dermatitidis","Legionella species","Coxiella burnetii","Histoplasma capsulatum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hotel or cruise ship travel in the past two weeks is linked with Legionella species, which favors confined spaces and areas with circulating water droplets such as hot tubs or fountains. Legionella is one of the atypical pathogens. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 41, 24"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man hikes to 4,000 meters and develops headache and breathlessness. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.50 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 29 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 22 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Metabolic acidosis","Acute respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Chronic respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.50 is alkalemia, and the carbon dioxide of 29 is low and matches it (respiratory). Bicarbonate fell about 2 per 10 mm Hg (11 below 40 gives about 22), the acute response. High altitude drives hypoxia-induced hyperventilation. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 28-year-old pregnant woman with allergic asthma asks whether keeping her baby away from dust, pets and pollen will prevent the child from developing asthma. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Avoidance matters just for pet dander","Strict avoidance prevents asthma","Early exposure guarantees asthma","Avoidance does not lower the risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled allergens are common triggers and are implicated in sensitization, but avoiding allergens does not reduce the risk of developing asthma, and early exposure may even be protective by building tolerance. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman on targeted testing for tuberculosis infection has an interferon-gamma release assay reported as indeterminate. She has no symptoms. Which next test is most appropriate?","choices":["Repeat the assay or use a skin test","Treat as positive; start therapy","Three sputum specimens for culture","Treat as negative; no further testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An indeterminate, invalid or borderline interferon-gamma release assay is handled by repeating the assay or using a tuberculin skin test as clinically appropriate. An indeterminate result says nothing either way about infection, so another immune-response test is needed. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman who had a kidney transplant takes long-term prednisone and anti-rejection drugs. She has 10 days of progressive shortness of breath and a dry cough, and her chest radiograph shows diffuse bilateral perihilar infiltrates. Induced sputum microscopy and Pneumocystis molecular testing are negative, but suspicion remains high. Which test should be done next?","choices":["Expectorated sputum sample","Pulmonary function testing","Serum lactate dehydrogenase","Bronchoalveolar lavage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A negative induced specimen does not exclude Pneumocystis, and bronchoalveolar lavage is considered when induced sputum is negative and suspicion remains high. Her transplant, steroid use and perihilar infiltrates keep suspicion high, and the lavage sample goes for microscopy or molecular testing and other infections. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 61, 67, 69"},{"q":"Which chloride pattern goes with normal anion gap metabolic acidosis?","choices":["Chloride does not change","Hyperchloremic","Normochloremic","Hypochloremic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis is hyperchloremic, since chloride rises as bicarbonate falls. Elevated anion gap acidosis is normochloremic, because other anions replace the bicarbonate. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man with asthma takes ibuprofen for a sprained ankle. Within 20 minutes he develops a runny nose, red irritated eyes, facial flushing and wheezing. Which diagnosis explains the reaction?","choices":["Exercise-induced bronchospasm","Aspirin-sensitive asthma","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","Cardiac asthma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. About 1 to 5% of asthmatics worsen with aspirin, and patients with true aspirin sensitivity cross-react with non-selective anti-inflammatory drugs such as ibuprofen. Rhinorrhea, conjunctival irritation, facial flushing and wheezing within minutes of the dose is the described reaction. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 92, 93"},{"q":"What is the most common reason for poor symptom control in refractory asthma?","choices":["Non-compliance","Steroid-resistant asthma","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","Exercise-induced bronchospasm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Refractory asthma takes two forms, persistent symptoms with poor lung function or normal function with severe exacerbations; non-compliance is the most common reason for poor symptom control, and steroid-resistant asthma is very uncommon. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has a chest radiograph showing fibrotic upper-lobe scarring that suggests prior tuberculosis, and he has never been treated for it. His tuberculin skin test shows 6 mm of induration. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Positive; his cut-off is 5 mm","Negative; old scars cause false results","Negative; his cut-off is 10 mm","Negative; his cut-off is 15 mm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Imaging suggesting prior tuberculosis places him in the 5 mm group, together with human immunodeficiency virus, recent close contact, transplant and major immunosuppression. 6 mm meets that cut-off, and his fibrotic lesion also warrants review of prior treatment and exclusion of active disease. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 22, 29"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man is found unresponsive at a party with pinpoint pupils and a respiratory rate of 6. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.24 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 62 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 26 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is present?","choices":["Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Chronic respiratory acidosis","Acute respiratory acidosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.24 is acidemia, and the carbon dioxide of 62 is high, which matches it, so the problem is respiratory. Bicarbonate rose only about 1 per 10 mm Hg (22 above 40 gives about 26), the acute response. A slow rate of 6 fits central nervous system depression. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic lung disease has been vomiting for days. His bicarbonate is 40 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L), his pH 7.46 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), and his carbon dioxide 58 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). What does the carbon dioxide level indicate?","choices":["A primary metabolic acidosis","A concurrent primary respiratory acidosis","Expected compensation alone","A concurrent respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In metabolic alkalosis, respiratory compensation rarely raises carbon dioxide above 55 mm Hg. His 58 is above that (and above 52, the 16 x 0.75 rise from 40), so a concurrent primary respiratory acidosis fits, along with his chronic lung disease. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with chronic lung disease has thick secretions she struggles to cough up during a severe exacerbation. Besides medication delivery, which listed rationale for a nebulizer applies?","choices":["Secretion clearance","Replacing her need for oxygen","Delivering a fixed oxygen fraction","Preventing carbon dioxide retention"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her thick secretions that she cannot cough up in a severe exacerbation match secretion clearance, which is a listed rationale for a nebulizer along with delivering liquid medication directly to the airways. The nebulizer helps move the mucus. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman who is 14 weeks pregnant comes in during October and asks about vaccines. Which vaccine should be avoided during her pregnancy?","choices":["Recombinant influenza vaccine","Tdap during this pregnancy","Inactivated influenza vaccine","Live attenuated influenza vaccine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In pregnancy, influenza vaccination uses an age-appropriate inactivated or recombinant vaccine in any trimester, and the live attenuated vaccine is avoided. Live attenuated vaccine has pregnancy and immunocompromise restrictions, so her pregnancy rules it out. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 5, 6, 124"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a resting oxygen saturation of 91% on room air, and she stays at 91% during a walking test. She asks for home oxygen. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["No long-term oxygen is indicated","She needs oxygen over 15 hours a day","She needs a sleep study first","She needs oxygen during exertion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A resting saturation of 89% to 93% is moderate resting desaturation, with no indication for long-term oxygen, and her saturation stays at 91% with exertion. Oxygen is indicated with severe hypoxemia, an arterial oxygen pressure under 55 mmHg or saturation of 88% or less. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man with asthma has symptoms 1 day a week, wakes at night once a month, uses albuterol 1 day a week, and has a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 91% predicted. In the past year, however, he needed 3 separate courses of oral corticosteroids for exacerbations. How should his asthma be classified?","choices":["As intermittent asthma","As persistent asthma","As well-controlled with no change","As exercise-induced bronchospasm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His impairment is intermittent-level, but patients with 2 or more exacerbations requiring oral systemic corticosteroids in the past year may be considered the same as patients with persistent asthma, even without persistent-level impairment. Three steroid courses put him in the persistent risk row. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 15-year-old soccer player develops cough and wheezing within minutes of hard running, peaking about 10 minutes later and resolving within an hour. She is otherwise symptom-free. Which treatment should she use before exercise?","choices":["Long-acting beta-2 agonist alone","Tiotropium","A short oral corticosteroid course","Inhaled short-acting beta-2 agonist"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her symptoms begin within minutes of exercise, peak at 10 to 15 minutes and resolve by an hour, the exercise-induced bronchospasm pattern. An inhaled short-acting beta-2 agonist used before exercise decreases the bronchoconstriction. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 94, 96"},{"q":"Which is the most sensitive routine diagnostic test for coronavirus disease 2019?","choices":["Nucleic acid amplification","Chest computed tomography","A D-dimer level","A single antigen test"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nucleic acid amplification, including polymerase chain reaction, is the most sensitive routine diagnostic method, using an assay-validated specimen. One negative antigen test does not exclude infection. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman vomits and aspirates during a witnessed seizure. Two hours later she has abrupt shortness of breath and hypoxemia, a temperature of 100.6°F and a white blood cell count of 13,500/µL. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Antibiotic with anaerobic coverage","Ampicillin-sulbactam","Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin","Supportive care, no antibiotic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A witnessed aspiration of gastric contents followed within hours by abrupt respiratory decline is aspiration pneumonitis, a sterile chemical injury; fever or leukocytosis can occur without bacterial infection. Support oxygenation and the airway, and avoid prophylactic antibiotics when bacterial infection is not suspected. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 50, 51, 126"},{"q":"A 2-year-old girl lives with her grandmother, who was just diagnosed with infectious pulmonary tuberculosis. The girl is well, and her first tuberculin skin test shows 0 mm of induration. Which next step is most appropriate?","choices":["Repeat testing in 1 year","The 17-week rifapentine regimen","Prompt public-health assessment","Reassurance that she is uninfected"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Early infection can produce a negative skin test, so one negative result soon after exposure does not settle it. Young children and immunocompromised contacts need prompt public-health assessment, and infancy and young childhood carry a higher risk of progression after infection. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 15, 23"},{"q":"What does the modified Medical Research Council dyspnea scale grade?","choices":["Four-year survival","Exacerbation risk","Airflow obstruction","Breathlessness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The modified Medical Research Council scale grades breathlessness from 0 (breathless only with strenuous exercise) to 4 (too breathless to leave the house, or breathless dressing). A grade of 2 or more counts as more symptoms. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 24, 52"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man on home oxygen has a gas water heater with a pilot light in the hallway where he likes to sit. How far from it should his oxygen be kept?","choices":["At least 2 feet","At least 5 feet","At least 10 feet","At least 50 feet"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Home oxygen teaching is to keep oxygen at least 10 feet from heat sources, which include pilot lights, electric appliances and candles. A gas water heater with a pilot light sits in the hallway where he sits, so his oxygen must be kept at least 10 feet away. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man who had a kidney transplant takes prednisone. He has a cough and a new lung nodule, and his serum cryptococcal antigen is positive. Over the past 2 days he has developed a headache and new confusion. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Repeat chest film in 6 weeks","Lumbar puncture","Fluconazole and observe","Urine Histoplasma antigen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cryptococcosis is evaluated for central nervous system and disseminated disease, often with lumbar puncture. His transplant and steroid use raise his risk, and a new headache with confusion signals possible central nervous system spread, which changes him to amphotericin-based induction. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man with confirmed chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a symptom assessment test score of 24 and a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 3. He had 3 moderate exacerbations in the past year. Using the 2020 four-group scheme, which group is he in?","choices":["Group D","Group C","Group B","Group E"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A symptom score of 24 and a dyspnea grade of 3 mean more symptoms, and 3 moderate exacerbations mean high risk. In the 2020 scheme, more symptoms with high risk is group D (group E in the current scheme). — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 35, 36, 41"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man with long-standing asthma has had several exacerbations this year despite good inhaler technique and compliance. Testing shows a strong hypersensitivity to Aspergillus fumigatus with markedly raised immunoglobulin E and immunoglobulin G antibodies to the mold. Which diagnosis explains his worsening?","choices":["Non-compliance with the controller","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","Steroid-resistant asthma","Occupational asthma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis is a hypersensitivity reaction to Aspergillus fumigatus that occurs almost exclusively in asthma or cystic fibrosis; exposure causes a vigorous immunoglobulin E and G response that produces acute asthma symptoms and exacerbations, as in this patient. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has had mild asthma since childhood, controlled with occasional albuterol. He asks whether his asthma is likely to become severe as he ages. Which reply is most accurate?","choices":["Most mild asthma turns severe within a decade","Severity rises steadily with each decade","Mild asthma rarely becomes severe","Severe asthma usually begins mild"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Asthma severity rarely varies for a given patient: mild asthma rarely becomes severe, and severe asthma is usually severe at onset. His long history of mild disease predicts it will most likely stay mild. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman is admitted for repair of a hip fracture. On hospital day 4 she develops fever, purulent sputum and a new infiltrate that was not on her admission chest radiograph. She has not been intubated. How should this pneumonia be classified?","choices":["Ventilator-associated pneumonia","Aspiration pneumonitis","Hospital-acquired pneumonia","Community-acquired pneumonia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hospital-acquired pneumonia develops 48 hours or more after admission and was not incubating on admission. Her new infiltrate appeared on day 4 after a clear admission film, and she was never intubated, so the ventilator label does not apply. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man treated for right lower lobe community-acquired pneumonia is worse on day 4 of appropriate antibiotics. His chest radiograph now shows a patchy, nonhomogeneous opacity, and atelectasis hides the lower lobes. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Stop antibiotics and observe","Repeat radiograph in 6 weeks","Add a second antibiotic","Chest computed tomography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Worsening on therapy calls for reassessing the diagnosis and looking for an abscess, empyema or another complication. Computed tomography follows the radiograph when the opacity is not homogeneous or atelectasis prevents seeing the lower lobes, and it finds the complication or alternative cause that changes drainage or treatment. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 37, 47, 125, 126"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with no medical conditions has community-acquired pneumonia and is stable for outpatient treatment. She has no risk for resistant organisms, and local pneumococcal resistance to macrolides is 30%. Which antibiotic is most appropriate?","choices":["Cefpodoxime plus azithromycin","Amoxicillin","Azithromycin as a single drug","Levofloxacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With no comorbidities and no resistant-organism risk, the outpatient choices are amoxicillin or doxycycline. Macrolide monotherapy is used only if local pneumococcal resistance is below 25%, and hers is 30%, so amoxicillin fits. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has 10 days of steadily worsening breathlessness and cough after a viral illness. Which term describes her presentation?","choices":["Disease progression","Acute bronchitis","Acute exacerbation","Stable group B disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An acute exacerbation is an acute worsening of dyspnea, cough or sputum over 14 days or less, commonly triggered by viral or bacterial infection or pollution. Her 10-day decline after a viral illness fits, once pneumonia, embolism, acute heart failure and pneumothorax are excluded. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 70-year-old unvaccinated man with diabetes and heart failure has mild coronavirus disease 2019 that began 6 days ago. His oxygen saturation is 96% on room air. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Dexamethasone","Nirmatrelvir-ritonavir","Molnupiravir","A 3-day remdesivir course"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A high-risk outpatient with mild or moderate disease and no hypoxemia is offered nirmatrelvir/ritonavir within 5 days or a three-day intravenous remdesivir course within 7 days of symptom onset. At day 6 only the remdesivir window is still open, and his age, comorbidities and lack of vaccination make him high risk. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 113, 120, 122"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man is admitted to a general ward with nonsevere community-acquired pneumonia and no resistant-organism risk. Macrolides and fluoroquinolones are unsuitable for him because of severe reactions to each class. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Ceftriaxone plus doxycycline","Moxifloxacin monotherapy","Ceftriaxone plus vancomycin","Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nonsevere inpatient pneumonia is usually treated with a beta-lactam plus a macrolide, or with respiratory fluoroquinolone monotherapy. A beta-lactam plus doxycycline is the alternative when macrolides and fluoroquinolones are unsuitable, as his reactions make them. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease had 2 moderate exacerbations and 1 hospital admission in the past year. His blood eosinophil count is 350. He is starting maintenance therapy. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["A single bronchodilator","Triple inhaled therapy","An inhaled corticosteroid alone","Daily oral corticosteroids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two moderate exacerbations and an admission make him high risk, group E. Group E starts on a long-acting beta agonist plus a long-acting antimuscarinic, and adding an inhaled corticosteroid is considered when blood eosinophils are 300 or more, as his 350 are. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 37, 42"}]};
